## Threats considered

### T1 — Exported callable decoys (dlsym / interposition)

**Threat:** If decoys were global `void __oo_meta_decoy_N(void)`, an attacker
(or accidental link with `-rdynamic` / plugin load) could resolve and call them;
names become a stable public ABI gadget surface.

**Finding (pre-fix):** Emit already used `static void` (internal linkage).

**Mitigation:** Keep **file-local `static`**. Never emit non-static decoys.
Names stay `__oo_meta_decoy_<seq>` (not user-derived). Documented in emit
security contract. **Status: mitigated.**

### T2 — Main-like / ctor / entry collision

**Threat:** Decoy named `main`, `_start`, `_init`, or marked
`constructor`/`destructor` becomes an unintended entry / auto-run gadget.

**Finding:** Names are fixed prefix `__oo_meta_decoy_` + decimal sequence.
No `__attribute__((constructor))`, no section tricks for .init_array.

**Mitigation:** Forbidden name classes called out in `c_emit_meta.oo` contract;
emit path never interpolates source identifiers into decoy symbols.
**Status: mitigated.**

### T3 — Function-pointer tables / indirect call gadgets

**Threat:** Storing decoy addresses in a static array / vtable creates
writable or readable call targets (CFI-bypass / ROP staging).

**Finding:** Path-A emit takes **no addresses**, builds **no fptr tables**.

**Mitigation:** Hard ban in security contract: no function pointers in decoys.
Keep-alive uses `__attribute__((used,noinline))` only (no address materialization
in generated C). **Status: mitigated.**

### T4 — system / exec / spawn sinks inside decoys

**Threat:** “Harmless” padding that calls `system`, `exec*`, `popen`, or
runtime `oo_sys_exec` becomes a planted shell gadget if ever reached.

**Finding:** Decoy body is only a volatile unsigned store + void cast.

**Mitigation:** No libc process APIs, no oo_sys_*, no I/O in decoy emit.
**Status: mitigated.**

### T5 — Format-string / user-controlled string into decoy text

**Threat:** If decoy names or bodies concatenated source identifiers, paths,
or format templates (`printf(user)`), source becomes an inject/format surface
in generated C.

**Finding:** Body embeds only `idx` via `Int.to_string()` (emit sequence).
Negative idx clamped to 0. No `printf`/`scanf` in decoy C.

**Mitigation:** Sequence-only decimal; never source spans, paths, or env.
**Status: mitigated.**

### T6 — Opt-in marker as silent layout/hash footgun

**Threat:** Accidental or adversarial addition of `// METAMORPHIC: path-a`
changes object layout and disk hash vs default product builds (supply-chain /
repro surprise). Mid-line / string occurrence might false-enable.

**Finding:** Parser is **line-start only** (`i==0` or prior `\n`). Default OFF.
Tension with deterministic builds is DESIGN-explicit (opt-in).

**Mitigation:** Line-start match; default off; docs + residual free-names for
full metamorphic APIs. Residual: marker still opt-in by anyone who can write
source (expected for a compiler directive). **Status: accepted / residual
(supply-chain policy, not gadget).**

### T7 — Dead-code elimination kills diversity (feature death, not RCE)

**Threat:** `-O2`/`LTO` discards unused `static` functions → path-A becomes a
no-op while still advertising layout diversity.

**Finding:** Pre-fix body had no keep-alive; DCE likely.

**Mitigation (OWN fix):** Emit GCC/clang `__attribute__((used,noinline))`
around static decoys so pads survive optimization **without** export or fptrs.
**Status: mitigated (feature keep); residual ROP entropy still weak.**

### T8 — Residual ROP / code-reuse on decoy bodies

**Threat:** Even static, kept functions are bytes in `.text`. An exploit with
a controlled PC may ret into decoy prologues/epilogues (classic ROP).

**Finding:** Body is minimal (volatile store immediate + return). Low gadget
richness vs real product code; still not zero.

**Mitigation:** Minimize instructions; no indirect branches; no useful
syscalls. **Honesty residual:** decoys are **not** CFI, **not** call-graph
integrity (`sign_callgraph` / `verify_callgraph` still residual refuse).
**Status: residual (inherent to any .text).**

### T9 — Overclaim: “immune system” / continuous mutation

**Threat:** Marketing or agent docs treat path-A as runtime polymorphic
binary / ASLR replacement / CFI, leading to wrong threat models.

**Finding:** DESIGN correctly labels path-A partial; full mutation residual.
`meta_epoch` is process-local random fixed at first call — **not** re-morph
of live assembly. Emit decoys are **deterministic per source order** (same
source → same decoy indices); process ASLR still provides base slide.

**Mitigation / required honesty statement:**

> **Layout diversity (path-A decoys) is a weak ASLR-style helper only.
> It is not control-flow integrity (CFI), not continuous runtime re-mutation,
> and not a substitute for OS ASLR, stack canaries, or W^X.**

**Status: residual documented; claim sealed to path-A floor.**

### T10 — `meta_epoch` entropy quality / side channel

**Threat:** Weak seed → predictable “future immune” hooks; epoch leak via
println aids layout prediction if epoch ever drove layout (today it does not
drive decoy emit).

**Finding:** Prefers `getentropy`; fallback mixes address of static, pid,
monotonic us. Not claimed CSPRNG for crypto. Epoch not wired into decoy
generation (emit is deterministic sequence).

**Mitigation:** Residual: not CSPRNG / attested entropy. Do not use epoch as
crypto key material. **Status: residual (honest floor).**

### T11 — Residual free-name bypass

**Threat:** `metamorphic_emit` / `metamorphic_build` / `metamorphic_vs_det`
ship as real codegen → false product immune APIs.

**Finding:** `check_residual.oo` maps them to `METAMORPHIC` / `META_VS_DET`
and default-denies at check. Smoke expects refuse.

**Mitigation:** Keep residual refuse; do not implement stubs that pretend full
immune product. **Status: mitigated (path-A refuse).**

### T12 — Emit pipeline staleness / dual-green gap

**Threat:** Source has path-A; product `oodac` binary may predate it
(`emit-c` fixture failed on stale binary during this audit: unknown free call
`meta_epoch`). Agents may claim green while binary lag hides decoys.

**Mitigation:** Smoke already soft-skips live emit when binary lags; rebuild
required for dual-green. **Status: residual process / rebuild — not a gadget.**

